Syed Ali Zaidi, 28, Quetta; Second room; Go to;

THIRD ROOM

As the jingle signifying the end of the news report plays, you turn off the radio. Your eyes are heavy but you promise yourself a hot cup of tea and a long sleep just as soon as you reach home. There were the usual cases in the ER last night: road traffic casualties, a businessman with a heart attack, and a suicide attempt. All patched up and fairly stable by the time you signed off in the morning. 

The news, though, now that was troubling. A part of your mind was focused on the report, worrying, turning it over again and again, thinking about the consequences and the implications. Conditions were terrible in Pakistan these days. It was best to just get out of the country alive while one still could. But you knew that wasn’t an option for you. 

It wasn’t that a doctor of your standing couldn't find a position abroad; it was simply a deep streak of patriotism and a sense of obstinacy that kept you sticking here. And your mother of course. Adjusting to new ways would be difficult for her. Speaking of which, you had to pick her up today.

You take the turning back to the school near your house and wait for your mother as the bell inside rings and the children come flooding out. 

FOURTH ROOM

"Assalam-alaikum Beta", she said affectionately, as she sat down in the car. "You seem really tired today."

"Walaikum Salam Maa", you reply. She was partly correct. You were tired, but you were also worried. However, it made no sense to worry her too. 

You were the only child of your mother, who was widowed while you were still eight years old. For the past twenty years, she had spent her days teaching at a local school in the mornings and at a tuition institute in the evenings to ensure that you got a good education.

It was not an easy job. She had to navigate the treacherous waters of the society with constant vigilance, lest people point fingers at her. Her struggle bore fruit, and you became a doctor, like she had always dreamed. She still taught, though, out of love for the job and a desire to stay active. And she still fussed over you as she did when you were a child. You were the centre of all her hopes and dreams and efforts then, as you are now. 

FIFTH ROOM

The next day you arrive at the hospital to find a note from the supervisor. You had been called to his office. While you are waiting outside, you see your fellow resident, a young man who boasted more of resources than skills at doctoring, leave the room with an unctuous smile on his face. 

As you enter the office, the supervisor is hastily shoving an envelope into a drawer of his desk and closing it. You wait until he straightens up. He seems redder in the face than usual, and his demeanour, never very encouraging or open to begin with, seems colder. 

He shuffles around papers on his desk, ignoring you long enough to make you uncomfortable. When you clear your throat, he looks up at you abruptly. 

"Dr Zaidi, we have decided not to take you on for the senior post graduate registrar position this year. Based on your performance, we feel it would be best if you continued a year more as a senior house officer. When you have enough experience you may be promoted to the post."

You feel your heart sink, and anger rise up. You debate asking him who HAD gotten the position.

Ask/don't ask

"The position has been given to Dr. Umar. He is a fine young gentleman who has shown persuasively excellent performance throughout."

No wonder the fellow resident looked so smarmy. He must have pulled some strings, or pulled out his wallet anyhow. You feel the anger turning to rage, and exit the room before you end up making a scene.

