SIXTH ROOM

One week later. 

It is evening, and you have just woken up from confused dreams. Your mother is standing over you, shaking you awake gently. 

"Beta, there is news of a blast in the city’s main Imam Baargah. The victims will be arriving at your hospital any time soon. You ought to be there."

You feel energy suffuse into your limbs; clearing away the sleepiness, you get up and get dressed hurriedly, hugging your mother on the way out. You knew it broke her heart to disturb your rest, but she is aware how much it means to you to help people, and after a blast the hospital needed as many hands as it could get. You leave quickly. 

SEVENTH ROOM

One month later: 

You are heading back from your evening duty one night. You have only just noticed a van that seems to be tailing you and have begun to feel suspicious when it swerves to the side and in front of you, forcing you to stop. Four men step out. 

They come close to your car. One of them, wearing a black cloth over the lower half of his face, taps the window of your car with his gun. Open up, he signs. Terrified, you do so. He pulls you out of the car roughly into the ditch at the side of the road. 

Two of the men push you onto your knees. They shout at you to put your hands behind your head. You beg them to take anything, the car, the mobile phone, the measly amount of money in your wallet. One of them rifles your pockets, but they are not looking for money. They check your ID card.

“You are Dr. Ali Zaidi? Shi’a?”

As you hear the sound of the gun being cocked, you realize what they were really after. You go cold. The last noise you hear is the bullet exploding from the barrel, the last thought you have is of your mother, waiting late into the night, while the lovingly cooked food grows cold on the dinner table.